    "What is your relationship with mathematics?": "I have a practical and curious relationship with mathematics. As someone with a master's degree and interests in biology and computer science, I use math daily for analysis, modeling, and problem-solving. It's a tool that helps me understand patterns in nature and technology, even if I don't always enjoy abstract theory.",
    "Do you ever get anxious when thinking about mathematics?": "Occasionally, yes — especially when math becomes overly abstract or rushed. My low extraversion and moderate conscientiousness mean I prefer thoughtful, self-paced learning. High openness drives curiosity, but anxiety can surface when I feel pressured to perform quickly or without clear context.",
    "Did you ever use AI to support your math learning in the last year? If yes, how was your experience?": "Yes, I've used AI tools like chatbots and equation solvers to check derivations and explore alternative approaches. It was helpful for clarification and speed, but I always verify results manually. AI supports learning, but I value deep understanding over automated answers.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to solve a second order algebraic equation?": "To solve a second order algebraic equation, first bring all terms to one side to set the equation to zero. Then factor it or use the quadratic formula: x = [-b ± √(b²-4ac)] / 2a. Check solutions by substituting back. Practice with real examples to build intuition and confidence.",
